
KUSBH - Klipper USB Helper
==========================

A small helper to move gcode between a USB flash drive and Klipper,
and to back up the Klipper configuration to USB.


!! WARNING !!

Do not use the USB flash drive while printing.
A file with the same name as the one being printed could be
overwritten or deleted.


Features
========

1. COPY  - inserting a USB drive containing one or more "gcode" files
   copies them into the print folder.

2. MOVE  - inserting an empty USB drive moves all "gcode" files
   from the print folder onto the drive.

3. BACKUP - inserting a USB drive that contains a "backup" folder
   backs up the Klipper configuration (config) and database
   (database) onto the drive. Each backup goes into its own
   timestamped subfolder, so previous backups are never
   overwritten:
       backup/2025-09-04_19-33-01/

   If a "backup" folder is present, ONLY the backup runs
   (gcode import/export is skipped).

   To back up more, add the folder name to the
   "for item in config database" list in the "mountcopy" file.


Configuration
=============

You do NOT need to set the path to "printer_data" - the script
finds it automatically (it expects Klipper's standard layout
/home/<user>/printer_data, so it works for pi, biqu, mks, etc.).


Install from GitHub (KIAUH style)
=================================

One command (downloads the repo and launches the menu):

   bash <(curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/drumclock/KUSBH/main/bootstrap.sh)

Or the classic way:

   cd ~
   git clone https://github.com/drumclock/KUSBH.git
   ./KUSBH/install.sh


Menu (install.sh)
=================

   1) Install
   2) Uninstall
   3) Update  (git pull)
   4) Status


Manual install
==============

1. sudo cp 99-mountcopy.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
2. sudo cp mountcopy /usr/bin
   sudo chmod +x /usr/bin/mountcopy
3. sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger


Manual uninstall
================

1. sudo rm /etc/udev/rules.d/99-mountcopy.rules
2. sudo rm /usr/bin/mountcopy
3. sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
